          A desiccant dehumidifier is the right tool for one specific job: pulling water out of cold air. Below about 60°F a normal compressor dehumidifier loses most of its capacity, and near 40°F it spends half its time defrosting. A desiccant rotor does not care. The trade is power and heat: these machines draw more watts per pint than a compressor unit in a warm room, and they blow out noticeably warm air. The choice on Amazon is also thinner than most roundups admit, so this guide picks the four genuine rotor desiccant units that are actually in stock, from a $110 budget model to Ivation's 19 pint garage machine, and explains where each one earns its electricity.
