        <div id="pint-ratings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Reading the Pint Rating on a Desiccant Unit
          </h2>

          <p>
            Desiccant capacity numbers are smaller than compressor numbers, and that is mostly honest. A 19 pint desiccant unit rated at 68°F will still be pulling something close to that at 45°F, while a 35 pint compressor unit rated at 65°F may be pulling a fraction of it. Comparing the two pint figures directly understates the desiccant unit in the cold and overstates it in the warm. The useful habit is to ask what temperature a rating was measured at and what temperature your space actually is.
          </p>

          <p>
            The confusing labels come from the other direction. Several desiccant brands sell their machines under a compressor-equivalent name. Ecor Pro's EPD50-PRO is marketed as a 50 pint dehumidifier, but its own published figures are 25 pints per day under the older DOE test and 21 pints at AHAM conditions. The 50 is Ecor Pro's claim about how much a compressor unit would need to be rated at to match it in cold conditions. That is not necessarily wrong, but it is not a measurement, and you should size on the 21 to 25 pint figures.
          </p>

          <p>
            Metric ratings add another wrinkle. European-designed units such as the Eva-Dry are rated in liters per day at 20°C and 60% humidity, which is 68°F. Seven liters is about 14.8 US pints. If a listing quotes an imperial pint figure from a UK source, it will look slightly smaller than the US equivalent, because an imperial pint is about 20% larger. Converting everything to US pints at a stated temperature is the only way to line up the options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, be wary of cheap rotary units that promise 1,000 or 1,200 square feet from a 10 pint rating. Coverage claims on desiccant machines are even less meaningful than on compressor units, because the real limit is how much moisture the space gains, not its floor area. A tight, insulated 400 square foot room may need very little; a leaky 200 square foot boat shed in coastal fog may need far more. Size on the moisture load, and treat any coverage number as a rough upper bound.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-and-heat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Wattage, Running Cost and the Warm Exhaust
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every desiccant dehumidifier is partly a heater, and the wattage on the label tells you how much. The Ivation 13-pint is listed at 470 watts on turbo and 280 on regular, the Ivation 19-pint at about 670 watts, the Eva-Dry at 615 and the AEOCKY X1-Pro at 340. Industrial ducted units run higher still; Ebac lists its DD200 at 800 watts. Because the heater is regenerating the wheel continuously while the unit runs, these figures are close to what the machines actually draw, not a peak you rarely see.
          </p>

          <p>
            That electricity does not disappear. In a tank-style unit, almost all of it ends up as heat in the room, together with the heat released when the water condenses. A 670-watt machine adds roughly 2,300 BTU per hour to the space, about a third of a small plug-in space heater on high. Owners commonly describe the exhaust as noticeably warm, and Ivation's own guide notes that warm, dry output air is normal for a desiccant unit rather than a fault.
          </p>

          <p>
            In the spaces these machines are built for, that is a feature. Warming a cold garage or cabin a few degrees lowers its relative humidity even before any water is removed, so the dehumidifier and its waste heat work together. In a summer bedroom or a room you are also air conditioning, the same heat works against you, and it is another reason not to use a desiccant unit where a compressor would do the job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Running cost follows directly. At the US average of roughly 17 cents per kWh, a 670-watt unit running eight hours a day uses about 5.4 kWh, or around 90 cents a day; on the 280-watt setting, the Ivation 13-pint would cost under 40 cents for the same hours. A humidistat that switches the machine off once the space reaches 50% to 55% cuts that dramatically, which is why we would not buy a desiccant unit without one. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-electricity-does-a-dehumidifier-use/">how much electricity a dehumidifier uses</a> has the method for working out your own figure.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Listed Power Draw</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
