        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a desiccant dehumidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is a dehumidifier that removes moisture with a slowly turning wheel coated in an absorbent material such as silica gel or zeolite, instead of a cold refrigerant coil. A small heater dries the wheel continuously. Because it does not rely on chilling the air, it keeps working in cold spaces where compressor dehumidifiers lose most of their capacity or frost up.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are desiccant dehumidifiers better than compressor dehumidifiers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only in cold spaces. Below roughly 60°F a desiccant unit holds its output while a compressor unit falls away, so it is better for garages, boats, cabins and unheated rooms. Above about 65°F a compressor unit removes more water per watt and does not heat the room, so it is the better choice for heated basements and living areas.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do desiccant dehumidifiers use a lot of electricity?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They draw more per pint than a compressor unit in a warm room. The picks here are listed between 280 and 670 watts, and they draw close to that whenever they run. A humidistat that switches the unit off at 50% to 55% keeps running costs down, and in a cold space the waste heat is partly useful because it raises the temperature.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a desiccant dehumidifier work below freezing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some can. Ducted industrial desiccant units that push wet air outside, such as the Ecor Pro EPD50-PRO and Ebac DD200, are rated down to about -4°F, but they are expensive specialist machines. Tank-style domestic units such as the Ivation, Eva-Dry and AEOCKY models are rated only to about 33°F or 34°F, because the water they collect in the tank or drain hose would freeze.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do desiccant dehumidifiers heat the room?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Almost all of the electricity a tank-style desiccant unit uses ends up as heat in the space, so the exhaust air is noticeably warm. A 670-watt unit adds roughly 2,300 BTU per hour. That is welcome in a cold garage or cabin, where warmer air also means lower relative humidity, but unwelcome in an air-conditioned room in summer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size desiccant dehumidifier do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a closet, RV or single small room, about 13 pints per day is enough. For a one or two car garage or a larger unheated room, a 19 pint unit is the practical size. Large cold buildings, boat sheds and storage spaces need ducted industrial units. Size on how damp the space gets rather than on the square footage claims in listings.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are desiccant dehumidifiers good for boats?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, they are the usual choice for boats in winter storage, because the air in a closed hull stays cold and a compressor unit would barely work. Run one on shore power with a drain hose to a sink or cockpit drain. For a boat stored outdoors in freezing weather, you need a ducted marine unit rated below freezing, which usually means a specialist supplier rather than Amazon.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
